---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Badge } from "@/components/ui/badge";
---

<Layout title="GSoC 2020 — conda-forge auto-tick bot — Vinicius Cerutti">
  <div class="w-[min(1120px,calc(100%-44px))] mx-auto">
    <main>
      <header class="page-header">
        <div class="kicker">open source · 2020</div>
        <h1 class="page-title">conda-forge auto-tick bot</h1>
        <p class="page-sub">Google Summer of Code 2020. Separating the version-update pipeline from the conda-forge dependency graph into its own microservice.</p>
      </header>

      <section class="project-layout py-[clamp(48px,6vw,72px)]">
        <div class="project-main">
          <h2>Intent</h2>
          <p>
            The conda-forge auto-tick bot maintains thousands of conda packages automatically — pushing version bumps,
            running migrations, and keeping feedstocks in sync. Its core data structure is a graph where nodes are packages
            and payloads carry version state. The version-update logic lived inside that graph, making it hard to scale,
            test, or migrate to a different storage backend.
          </p>
          <p>
            The GSoC project was to extract the <code>update_upstream_versions</code> function into a standalone microservice
            that writes new package versions to a separate store — originally targeting DynamoDB — without touching the graph directly.
          </p>

          <h2>What the work involved</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Understanding the graph structure: nodes as packages, <code>LazyJson</code> payloads, and how the bot reads and writes state.</li>
            <li>Mapping the source handlers that resolve new versions — PyPI, CRAN, NPM, ROSDistro, RawURL, and GitHub release tags.</li>
            <li>Writing a version-fetch loop that checks each package independently and serializes results to JSON, decoupled from graph mutation.</li>
            <li>Contributing the <code>migrate_update_version.py</code> module and a corresponding PR to <code>cf-graph-countyfair</code>.</li>
            <li>Weekly meetings with conda-forge core members and mentors; first experience navigating a large, active open source project.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>What made it interesting</h2>
          <p>
            The bot processes thousands of packages daily. Any regression in the version-fetch path degrades the whole
            ecosystem. The boundary between "what belongs in the graph" and "what should live outside it" was not obvious —
            the work was as much about understanding those boundaries as it was about writing code.
          </p>
          <p>
            This was also a first experience contributing to a large community project: understanding PR review cycles,
            writing code others would read and maintain, and communicating design intent clearly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <aside class="project-side">
          <h3>stack</h3>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-6">
            {["python", "conda-forge", "dynamodb", "json", "github actions"].map(t => <Badge variant="outline">{t}</Badge>)}
          </div>
          <h3>themes</h3>
          <p>Microservice extraction, package version resolution, open source contribution, graph data structures.</p>
          <h3>links</h3>
          <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/regro/cf-scripts" style="color: var(--link); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 12px;">regro/cf-scripts →</a><br/>
            <a href="https://github.com/regro/cf-graph-countyfair" style="color: var(--link); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 12px;">cf-graph-countyfair →</a>
          </p>
          <a href="/projects/" class="back-link">← all projects</a>
        </aside>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-header {
    padding: clamp(64px, 8vw, 108px) 0 clamp(36px, 4vw, 52px);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); max-width: 820px;
  }
  .kicker {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 11px; text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 18px;
  }
  .page-title {
    font-size: clamp(38px, 5.5vw, 68px); font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.065em; line-height: 0.97; margin: 0 0 18px; color: var(--foreground);
  }
  .page-sub { font-size: 19px; color: var(--muted-foreground); line-height: 1.55; margin: 0; }
  .project-layout { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 260px; gap: clamp(40px, 5vw, 64px); }
  .project-main h2 {
    margin: 36px 0 10px; font-size: 22px; font-weight: 650;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em; color: var(--foreground);
  }
  .project-main h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .project-main p, .project-main li { color: #c3cbd6; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.7; }
  .project-main ul { padding-left: 1.3rem; margin: 10px 0 18px; }
  .project-main li + li { margin-top: 7px; }
  .project-main code { color: #dfbb7f; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.88em; background: rgba(216,166,87,0.07); padding: 1px 5px; border-radius: 4px; }
  .project-side { border-left: 1px solid var(--border); padding-left: 26px; padding-top: 4px; }
  .project-side h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 12px; text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em; color: var(--faint); margin: 0 0 12px;
  }
  .project-side h3 + h3, .project-side div + h3, .project-side p + h3 { margin-top: 28px; padding-top: 28px; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .project-side p { font-size: 14px; color: var(--muted-foreground); line-height: 1.6; margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .back-link { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 12px; color: var(--link); display: block; margin-top: 28px; }
  .back-link:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  @media (max-width: 780px) {
    .project-layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .project-side { border-left: none; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); padding-left: 0; padding-top: 24px; }
  }
</style>
